module Core

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   A byte count as a human reads one - `1.5 MiB`, `11 MB`, `512 B`.
   ==========================================================================================

   `File` reports sizes and the allocator reports its arenas, and both would have open-coded the same
   loop: divide by a thousand-odd until the number is small, pick a suffix, decide how many places to
   keep. It is four lines each time and the fourth is the one everybody gets wrong, so it is here
   once with the decision written down.

   **Binary by default, and the `i` is not optional.** `1 KiB` is 1024 bytes and `1 KB` is 1000, and
   a program that means the first and writes the second is stating a number that is 2.4% wrong by the
   time it reaches a terabyte. So the two are separate settings with separate suffixes rather than
   one setting and a footnote: a memory report is binary, a disk vendor's figure is SI, and the text
   says which one it is.

   **The scale is chosen from the value and the places from the scale**, which is the rule this file
   exists to state:

   - the largest unit under which the number is below the base - so `5120` is `5.0 KiB` and not
     `0.0 MiB` or `5120 B`;
   - one decimal place when that number is below ten, and none at or above it - so `5120` keeps its
     `.0` and `11534336` is `11 MiB` rather than `11.0 MiB`.

   The second half is what makes a column of these readable: three significant figures everywhere,
   which is as much as anybody reads off a size and one more than `du -h` gives.

   Both halves are overridable and neither is a mode: `bytes(n, scale: ByteScale.Mega)` always writes
   megabytes, and `bytes(n, decimals: 3)` always writes three places. What a caller cannot ask for is
   a scale the number does not have digits for, because there is no such thing - a scale is a
   divisor, and dividing is defined at every size.

   **There is no reader.** Every other formatter in this directory has one and this cannot honestly
   have one: `1.5 MiB` names an interval of 1024 byte counts rather than a number, so a round trip
   through it is lossy by construction. A caller who needs the number back should not have been given
   the text.
-}

{-
   Which unit, as its own power of the base.

   `@value` is the exponent rather than declaration order, exactly as `Radix`'s is the base: the
   number *is* what the divisor is built from, so the declaration says it once and the loop below
   reads it rather than restating it in a `match`.

   `Auto` is last and above the run rather than `-1` in front of it, so every other value is the
   exponent it names and the sentinel is outside the range a loop counts over. It is a constructor
   and not a `Maybe(ByteScale)` field for `NumberStyle`'s reason: a style is a record of scalars,
   and one more constructor is cheaper to read than a wrapper at the field.

   Exa is the largest, because 1024^7 does not fit in sixty-four bits: a zettabyte cannot be counted
   in a `U64` of bytes, so a scale for it would be one no value could reach.
-}
pub data qualified ByteScale
  = @value(0) Bytes
  | @value(1) Kilo
  | @value(2) Mega
  | @value(3) Giga
  | @value(4) Tera
  | @value(5) Peta
  | @value(6) Exa
  | @value(7) Auto

{-
   How to write a byte count.

   `decimals` is a `Size` with `-1` for "decide from the value", which is the sentinel this style
   spends where `NumberStyle` spends zero on its separator: zero places is a real request - `bytes(n,
   decimals: 0)` is what a table of whole megabytes wants - so the absent value has to be a number
   below the range rather than the bottom of it.

   `binary` is a `Bool` and not a base, because there are two and only two: 1024 with the `i`
   suffixes and 1000 without. A caller wanting some other divisor is not writing byte counts.
-}
pub data ByteStyle {
    scale: ByteScale = ByteScale.Auto,
    decimals: Size = -1,
    binary: Bool = True
}

pub alias ByteFormat(a) = Formatted(ByteStyle, a)

pub fn byteStyle(scale: ByteScale = ByteScale.Auto, decimals: Size = -1, binary: Bool = True) -> ByteStyle =
    ByteStyle {scale: scale, decimals: decimals, binary: binary}

-- The SI form under its own name, because a call site that means disk-vendor gigabytes should say so
-- rather than passing `binary: False` and hoping a reader notices.
pub fn siByteStyle(scale: ByteScale = ByteScale.Auto, decimals: Size = -1) -> ByteStyle =
    ByteStyle {scale: scale, decimals: decimals, binary: False}

{-
   And the same applied to a number, which is what a call site writes.

   Generic over `Magnitude(a, w)` like `hex` and `number` above it, so a `Size` from `length`, a
   `USize` from an allocator and a `U64` from a file system all reach it without an ascription - and
   the arithmetic happens at that `w`, so a byte count that fits thirty-two bits is not a `BigInt` on
   JavaScript. A negative count is written with its sign: nothing here is about what a byte count
   ought to be, and a *difference* between two of them is the ordinary reason one is negative.

   The record literal rather than `with`, for `Core/Format.yana`'s reason - `with` takes its style as
   `'s` and a constructor handing back a `with` result would be returning a root into its own frame.
-}
pub fn (Magnitude(a, w)) bytes(value: 'a, scale: ByteScale = ByteScale.Auto, decimals: Size = -1, binary: Bool = True) -> ByteFormat(a) =
    Formatted {value: value, style: byteStyle(scale, decimals, binary)}

-- The suffix for one exponent, as the whole literal rather than a prefix joined to `iB`: `++` is an
-- allocation and a copy to produce three units the compiler already has as a constant.
fn scaleSuffix(step: Int, binary: Bool) -> String
  | {1, True}  -> "KiB"
  | {1, _}     -> "KB"
  | {2, True}  -> "MiB"
  | {2, _}     -> "MB"
  | {3, True}  -> "GiB"
  | {3, _}     -> "GB"
  | {4, True}  -> "TiB"
  | {4, _}     -> "TB"
  | {5, True}  -> "PiB"
  | {5, _}     -> "PB"
  | {6, True}  -> "EiB"
  | {6, _}     -> "EB"
  | _          -> "B"

instance (Magnitude(a, w), Show(w), Bounded(w), Integral(w), Ord(w), TrivialCopy(w), Narrow(w, Int), Narrow(Int, w)) ShowWith(ByteStyle, a):
    fn showWith(subject: ByteFormat(a), &to: String):
        let style = subject.style :: ByteStyle
        let base = truncate(if style.binary then 1024 else 1000) :: w
        let size = magnitude(subject.value)

        if isNegative(subject.value) then String.pushUnit(to, "-")

        {-
           The scale, as an exponent and the divisor built from it together.

           Multiplying up rather than dividing down, because the divisor has to exist at `w`: a
           `U32` byte count has no exabyte divisor, and building one to divide by would be the
           overflow this loop is written to avoid. The auto arm's test is its own guard - it steps
           only where the value is at least a base times the current divisor, so the next divisor is
           at or below the value and cannot wrap - and the explicit arm carries the same test
           written against the type's maximum, so a `U32` asked for exabytes writes the largest
           scale it has rather than dividing by zero.
        -}
        let &step = 0 :: Int
        let &scale = 1 :: w

        if style.scale is ByteScale.Auto:
            while step < 6 && size / scale >= base:
                scale = scale * base
                step = step + 1
        else:
            let wanted = valueOf(style.scale)
            while step < wanted && scale <= (maxValue() :: w) / base:
                scale = scale * base
                step = step + 1

        {-
           The number, the places and the rounding - recomputed where the rounding carries into the
           next scale up.

           A loop rather than a straight line, and it is the only way to keep the two rules honest
           at their own boundary: `1073741823` bytes is `1023.9999 MiB`, which rounds to `1024 MiB`,
           which is not a rendering anybody wants - and the answer is not `1.0 GiB` with the *first*
           scale's place count either, because the place count is chosen from the number and the
           number just changed. So the scale is bumped and the whole computation is redone, which
           runs at most six times and nearly always once.
        -}
        let &whole = 0 :: w
        let &frac = 0 :: w
        let &places = 0 :: Size
        let &power = 1 :: w
        let &carried = True

        while carried:
            carried = False
            whole = size / scale
            let rest = size % scale

            -- The places, from the style or from the number. A count at the bottom scale is a count
            -- of whole bytes and has no fraction to write, whatever either of them says.
            let wanted = if:
                style.decimals >= 0    -> style.decimals
                step > 0 && whole < 10 -> 1
                else                   -> 0

            {-
               Ten to the power of the places, clamped to what the divisor can express.

               `scale / power` is what a place is worth and it has to be at least one, so the loop
               stops where the next power would pass the divisor - which is what keeps `bytes(n,
               decimals: 9)` at a kilobyte scale from asking for digits the divisor cannot
               distinguish. The test is written as `power <= scale / 10` rather than
               `power * 10 <= scale` because the product is the thing that would wrap.
            -}
            power = 1
            places = 0

            while places < wanted && power <= scale / 10:
                power = power * 10
                places = places + 1

            {-
               The fraction, rounded to nearest rather than truncated.

               Truncating is what a duration's clock wants - a time that reads shorter than it is, is
               the direction a reader can act on - and it is the wrong answer for a size, where
               `1023.9 MiB` truncated to whole megabytes reads as a gibibyte less than it is at every
               scale above. So the halves round up, at every place count including none.

               **The remainder and the divisor are halved together until the multiply fits**, which
               is the one piece of arithmetic here that is not obvious and is the second thing this
               was written as. Dividing the divisor down first - `rest / (scale / power)` - needs no
               intermediate at all and is catastrophically wrong at exactly the case a byte count
               hits: `1536` at three places is `1024 / 1000 = 1`, so half a kibibyte reads as `.512`
               instead of `.500`. Multiplying first is exact and `rest * power` is what wraps, at a
               `U32` holding gibibytes.

               Halving both keeps the ratio and costs a relative error of one part in the divisor.
               The loop stops at the *first* shift that fits, so the divisor it stops at is above
               `max / (2 * power)` - a hundred thousand at the widest place count a caller can ask
               for at thirty-two bits, and far more at every other setting - which is orders below
               the last digit being written. Where no shift is needed, which is every value whose
               remainder is small against its own width, the answer is exact.

               The loop's own test has to be the whole of what the multiply needs, which is what it
               did not say in the first version: it left room for `num * power` and none for the
               half-divisor the rounding then added, and `3000000000` as a `USize` on JavaScript - a
               thirty-two-bit word there and a sixty-four-bit one natively - wrapped to `2.0 GiB` on
               one target and `2.8 GiB` on the other. The rounding is a comparison instead of an
               addition now, so the test and the arithmetic say the same thing.
            -}
            frac = 0

            if places > 0:
                let &num = rest
                let &den = scale

                while num > (maxValue() :: w) / power:
                    num = num / 2
                    den = den / 2

                let scaled = num * power
                let quotient = scaled / den
                let left = scaled % den
                let rounded = if left >= den - left then quotient + 1 else quotient

                if rounded >= power then whole = whole + 1
                else frac = rounded

            -- No places means the unit itself is the last digit, so the same rule applies to it.
            else if rest >= scale - rest then whole = whole + 1

            -- And a whole that reached the base is one of the next unit up. The guard on the divisor
            -- is what a value at the very top of its own width needs: a scale it cannot hold is one
            -- this stays below rather than wraps into.
            if whole >= base && step < 6 && scale <= (maxValue() :: w) / base && style.scale is ByteScale.Auto:
                step = step + 1
                scale = scale * base
                carried = True

        show(whole, to)

        if places > 0:
            String.pushUnit(to, ".")

            -- Most significant first, out of the power the places were counted into - the same
            -- running-divisor shape `Format/Number.yana`'s digit loop uses, and for the same reason:
            -- leading zeros are digits at their positions rather than a run in front of them.
            let &at = power / 10
            while at > 0:
                String.pushUnit(to, "0" + (truncate((frac / at) % 10) :: Int))
                at = at / 10

        String.pushUnit(to, " ")
        String.push(to, scaleSuffix(step, style.binary))

    {-
       A constant, and it is the *unscaled* number's width rather than the scaled one's.

       At the automatic scale the number written is below the base, so four digits would cover it -
       and a named scale is a divisor a caller may pick smaller than the value needs, so
       `bytes(n, scale: ByteScale.Bytes)` writes every digit the count has. Twenty is the widest
       decimal a sixty-four-bit magnitude reaches, and the first version of this answered eleven and
       was **short** at exactly that call, which is the one thing an instance may not be.

       Twenty digits, a sign, a point, the places, a space and three units of suffix. Generous by
       sixteen at the ordinary call, which is the trade every bound in this library makes, and the
       alternative would be a `showBound` that repeats the scale selection to be exact.
    -}
    fn showBoundWith(subject: ByteFormat(a)) -> Maybe(Size):
        let asked = subject.style.decimals
        return Just(26 + (if asked > 0 then asked else 1))
